      "pageTitle": "Magazines are back, and they’re newsletters.",
      "pageDescription": "Preeya Goenka on Beehiiv’s growing media portfolio.",
      "evidenceText": "Magazines Are Back, and They’re Newsletters - Columbia Journalism Review b   The Kicker b Sign up for the daily CJR newsletter . In early 2024, scores of journalists fled Substack over the company’s refusal to ban Nazi newsletters. Smaller newsletter companies saw an opening. Beehiiv, which was founded in 2021 by former Morning Brew employees, became one of the trendy upstarts, luring journalists such as Oliver Darcy and Ryan Broderick. Since then, Preeya Goenka—who has spent her career in email and joined Beehiiv in 2022—has expanded the company’s offerings for journalists. Those accepted into Beehiiv’s Media Collective pay no monthly fee for its service and receive legal support, a healthcare stipend, and audience-engagement help for two years. (It’s worth noting that journalists who aren’t part of that program have expressed frustration with Beehiiv’s rising monthly cost.) Most Media Collective members are still solo operators, but a growing number are running full newsrooms: Darcy now employs three journalists, while L.A. Material has a ten-person staff covering news across Los Angeles. I interviewed Goenka about how journalists fit into Beehiiv’s business strategy, who should consider starting a newsletter, and why Substack is definitely not the competition. Listen below, or wherever you get your podcasts. Show notes :  Beehiiv Media Collective L.A. Material Status   Garbage Day Credits : Host: Megan Greenwell Producer: Amanda Darrach Has America ever needed a media defender more than now? Help us by joining CJR today . Megan Greenwell is a freelance journalist based in Brooklyn and the author of Bad Company: Private Equity and the Death of the American Dream . More from CJR Is It Crazy to Go to Journalism School? A student reflects on leaving the law to pursue his writing dreams. By Megan Greenwell   Crooked Con’s Hottest Takes Meeting up with podcasters, creator-journalists, and political operatives against a dominant blue backdrop. By 