      "pageDescription": "24K likes, 153 comments - historyfeelsthepodcast on October 23, 2026: &quot;Hoover was born on August 10, 1874, in West Branch, Iowa, the son of a Quaker blacksmith. Andrew Johnson, Lincoln&#x2019;s successor, was still alive. He lived for nearly another year.\n\nHoover died on October 20, 1964, at age 90. By then, the next three presidents after Kennedy had already been born, and so had a three-year-old boy in Hawaii named Barack Obama.\n\nHis own life was remarkable long before the White House. Orphaned by age nine, he was part of Stanford University&#x2019;s very first graduating class. He became a mining engineer and was a millionaire by his 40s.\n\nDuring World War I, he organized food relief that helped feed millions of people in German-occupied Belgium, earning him the nickname &#x201c;th",
      "evidenceText": "Instagram {\"require\":[[\"ScheduledServerJS\",\"handle\",null,[{\"__bbox\":{\"require\":[[\"RelayPrefetchedStreamCache\",\"next\",[],[\"adp_PolarisLoggedOutDesktopWWWPostRootContentQueryRelayPreloader_6afd4ec90ac8b3145390839\",{\"__bbox\":{\"complete\":true,\"result\":{\"data\":{\"xig_polaris_media\":{\"__typename\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"pk\":\"4014573733054183961\",\"code\":\"De2oYpDTvoZ\",\"if_not_gated_logged_out\":{\"__typename\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"user\":{\"pk\":\"51334541463\",\"username\":\"historyfeelsthepodcast\",\"profile_pic_url\":\"https:\\/\\/scontent-dfw5-3.cdninstagram.com\\/v\\/t51.2885-19\\/327799364_650899300123499_2048669837137582164_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_s150x150_tt6&_nc_cat=1&ig_cache_key=GETSiRNrg5FN-U8CAFSYpNBcVm4cbkULAAAB1501500j-ccb7-5&ccb=7-5&_nc_sid=669407&efg=eyJ2ZW5jb2RlX3RhZyI6InByb2ZpbGVfcGljLnd3dy45MDAuQzMifQ\\u00253D\\u00253D&_nc_ohc=8OUPmdGnxhMQ7kNvwHj2ltx&_nc_oc=Adr283YxDNEzwzgoXaOeIUxbrd-uVouZGtgt5CySJz370L5qDaYqcnJPyo7kPaQ1X88&_nc_zt=24&_nc_ht=scontent-dfw5-3.cdninstagram.com&_nc_ss=7c689&_nc_ct=RQosPBUCFQIAAAA&oh=00_AQTq_UcEBg3p7hQosOmmw8vdWwsr2f16Q2dYBwEuYNQGZA&oe=6B030C73\",\"seo_canonical_url\":null,\"polaris_ordered_timeline_connection\":{\"edges\":[{\"node\":{\"__typename\":\"XIGPolarisCarouselMedia\",\"pk\":\"4032842659836573524\",\"__isXIGPolarisMedia\":\"XIGPolarisCarouselMedia\",\"accessibility_caption\":\"Photo by historyfeelsthepodcast on November 17, 2026.\",\"caption\":{\"text\":\"Bill Clinton turned 80 on August 19, 2026, joining George W. Bush, Donald Trump and Joe Biden in their 80s.\\n\\nBiden is the oldest, at 83, and he turns 84 on November 20. Obama, born in 1961, is 65.\\n\\nIt\\u2019s a sign of how much older American presidents have become. When John F. Kennedy took office in 1961, he was 43. Biden was 78 when he was sworn in in 2021, and Trump was 78 when he began his second term in 2025, the two oldest inaugurations in history.\\n\\nJimmy Carter, the longest-lived president ever, passed away in December 2024 at 100.\\n\\nAdded fact: Clinton, Bush and Trump were all born in the summer of 1946, within about 10 weeks of each other, at the start of the baby boom.\"},\"code\":\"Df3iQg-mbdU\",\"seo_canonical_url\":null,\"display_uri\":\"https:\\/\\/scontent-dfw5-1.cdninstagram.com\\/v\\/t51.82787-15\\/912405710_18086998685709464_1474084871402497983_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_e35_s640x640_tt6&_nc_cat=103&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ccb=7-5&_nc_sid=18de74&efg=eyJlZmdfdGFnIjoiQ0FST1VTRUxfSVRFTS5iZXN0X2ltYWdlX3VybGdlbi5DMyJ9&_nc_ohc=6ZAHfkqx8-0Q7kNvwEceW-b&_nc_oc=AdpRgNCAMO-2mxAHHtyKG7NL5F-0KBsRHCTrYb1TV4fwJXCZbgfzYts2tPdrct2e31o&_nc_zt=23&_nc_ht=scontent-dfw5-1.cdninstagram.com&_nc_gid=2f9KpDZMNOTDwnWGLfg1Hg&_nc_ss=7c689&_nc_ct=RQosPBUKFQIAAAA&oh=00_AQR0uihCnXc9d0IwTNQVXvRSsv8dGWlYDn3NobbFNRRLjA&oe=6B0330C6\",\"media_type\":8,\"product_type\":\"carousel_container\",\"user\":{\"pk\":\"51334541463\",\"username\":\"historyfeelsthepodcast\",\"id\":\"17841451283916817\"},\"carousel_media_count\":2,\"id\":\"POLARIS_4032842659836573524\"}},{\"node\":{\"__typename\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"pk\":\"4032707713318089972\",\"__isXIGPolarisMedia\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"accessibility_caption\":\"Photo by historyfeelsthepodcast on November 17, 2026. May be an image of artillery and text.\",\"caption\":{\"text\":\"Built by the German arms company Krupp, the gun had a barrel more than 100 feet long. Its shells flew so high, about 25 miles up, that they reached the edge of the stratosphere, higher than any human-made object until the V-2 rocket in World War II.\\n\\nEach shell took about three minutes to reach Paris. When the first ones landed in March 1918, Parisians thought they were being bombed by aircraft flying too high to see.\\n\\nThe deadliest strike came on March 29, 1918, Good Friday, when a shell hit the Saint-Gervais church during a service, killing nearly 90 people.\\n\\nThe gun was too inaccurate to hit specific targets. Its real purpose was to terrify the city and break French morale.\\n\\nAdded fact: As the war ended, the Germans destroyed the guns and their plans. Not a single Paris Gun survived, and historians still don\\u2019t know exactly how many were built.\"},\"code\":\"Df3DkyPTXT0\",\"seo_canonical_url\":null,\"display_uri\":\"https:\\/\\/scontent-dfw6-2.cdninstagram.com\\/v\\/t51.82787-15\\/910709536_18086998133709464_6306979452610343931_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_e35_s640x640_tt6&_nc_cat=101&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ccb=7-5&_nc_sid=18de74&efg=eyJlZmdfdGFnIjoiRkVFRC5iZXN0X2ltYWdlX3VybGdlbi5DMyJ9&_nc_ohc=zNg4fpXhtnoQ7kNvwGxhzc_&_nc_oc=Adroxzl7IYmz7cZURIR7qNfTPSC3o96PBD80lACpbM068YsP101s--5Y6rCl_Fku_ZE&_nc_zt=23&_nc_ht=scontent-dfw6-2.cdninstagram.com&_nc_gid=2f9KpDZMNOTDwnWGLfg1Hg&_nc_ss=7c689&_nc_ct=RQosPBUKFQIAAAA&oh=00_AQQ8iujxh97nVqq8WncAlZoaqBHTOb_ifdzd826n1e3Wsg&oe=6B030705\",\"media_type\":1,\"product_type\":\"feed\",\"user\":{\"pk\":\"51334541463\",\"username\":\"historyfeelsthepodcast\",\"id\":\"17841451283916817\"},\"id\":\"POLARIS_4032707713318089972\"}},{\"node\":{\"__typename\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"pk\":\"4032706875707237244\",\"__isXIGPolarisMedia\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"accessibility_caption\":\"Photo by historyfeelsthepodcast on November 17, 2026. May be an image of one or more people and text.\",\"caption\":{\"text\":\"On August 28, 1963, about 250,000 people gathered at the Lincoln Memorial to demand civil rights and economic justice for Black Americans. It was there that Martin Luther King Jr. delivered his \\u201cI Have a Dream\\u201d speech.\\n\\nNewman was one of many Hollywood stars who came to show their support, along with Marlon Brando, Charlton Heston, Sidney Poitier, Harry Belafonte and Burt Lancaster.\\n\\nAt the time, appearing at a civil rights march was a risk for a movie star, since it could cost them fans and roles. Newman went anyway.\\n\\nHe remained politically outspoken throughout his life, and in 1982, he co-founded Newman\\u2019s Own, a food company that donates all of its profits to charity. It has given away hundreds of millions of dollars.\\n\\nAdded fact: In the early 1970s, Newman appeared on President Richard Nixon\\u2019s infamous \\u201cenemies list.\\u201d He later called it his proudest achievement.\"},\"code\":\"Df3DYmJziN8\",\"seo_canonical_url\":null,\"display_uri\":\"https:\\/\\/scontent-dfw6-2.cdninstagram.com\\/v\\/t51.82787-15\\/912124711_18086997968709464_5150639237660211795_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_e35_s640x640_tt6&_nc_cat=106&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ccb=7-5&_nc_sid=18de74&efg=eyJlZmdfdGFnIjoiRkVFRC5iZXN0X2ltYWdlX3VybGdlbi5DMyJ9&_nc_ohc=7wlbSZ0bjGUQ7kNvwGMWXVR&_nc_oc=Adr-rChL7BEPxDHew0uaTQwNj6k2pcpNbZ9u5Z14fs8juK9GDe9bUfYuc-keyX2ppjg&_nc_zt=23&_nc_ht=scontent-dfw6-2.cdninstagram.com&_nc_gid=2f9KpDZMNOTDwnWGLfg1Hg&_nc_ss=7c689&_nc_ct=RQosPBUKFQIAAAA&oh=00_AQRU1Lggs8XXIAjanCIa3sf10xsp-msT3ThhNZZVxmKLrw&oe=6B030F2A\",\"media_type\":1,\"product_type\":\"feed\",\"user\":{\"pk\":\"51334541463\",\"username\":\"historyfeelsthepodcast\",\"id\":\"17841451283916817\"},\"id\":\"POLARIS_4032706875707237244\"}},{\"node\":{\"__typename\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"pk\":\"4032691962121249806\",\"__isXIGPolarisMedia\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"accessibility_caption\":\"Photo by historyfeelsthepodcast on November 17, 2026. May be an image of text.\",\"caption\":{\"text\":\"Thousands of years ago, speakers of an ancient language called Proto-Indo-European, the ancestor of most European and many Asian languages, used a word that linguists reconstruct as *kers-, meaning \\u201cto run.\\u201d\\n\\nFrom it came *kr\\u0325s\\u00f3s, meaning something like \\u201crunner\\u201d or \\u201cvehicle.\\u201d That word then split down two very different paths.\\n\\nIn the north, it may have become the Proto-Germanic *hruss\\u0105, meaning \\u201chorse.\\u201d Over centuries, that turned into the Old English \\u201chors\\u201d and, eventually, the modern English \\u201chorse.\\u201d\\n\\nIn the south, the Celts used it as *karros, meaning a wagon. The Romans borrowed it as \\u201ccarrus,\\u201d which became \\u201ccarre\\u201d in Old French, and finally \\u201ccar\\u201d in English.\\n\\nFor most of history, horses pulled the carts, so the \\u201crunner\\u201d and the vehicle were closely linked. Today, one word means the animal and the other means the machine that replaced it.\\n\\nAdded fact: The same ancient root gave English dozens of other words through the Latin \\u201ccurrere,\\u201d meaning \\u201cto run,\\u201d including \\u201ccareer,\\u201d \\u201ccourier,\\u201d \\u201ccurrent\\u201d and \\u201ccourse.\\u201d\"},\"code\":\"Df2__kyz2QO\",\"seo_canonical_url\":null,\"display_uri\":\"https:\\/\\/scontent-dfw5-2.cdninstagram.com\\/v\\/t51.82787-15\\/910383877_18086995760709464_6797404915709387266_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_e35_s640x640_tt6&_nc_cat=107&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ccb=7-5&_nc_sid=18de74&efg=eyJlZmdfdGFnIjoiRkVFRC5iZXN0X2ltYWdlX3VybGdlbi5DMyJ9&_nc_ohc=B1SS6dOIadcQ7kNvwEoi3EQ&_nc_oc=AdpWM-wEfVg6DoZ_PRyqd6lvuaU1pSfSuxLEYBI7-Wrabw2utoLA0Bg3NMJyUckJPR8&_nc_zt=23&_nc_ht=scontent-dfw5-2.cdninstagram.com&_nc_gid=2f9KpDZMNOTDwnWGLfg1Hg&_nc_ss=7c689&_nc_ct=RQosPBUKFQIAAAA&oh=00_AQSS3jeyx_OMja0zsZvj9jWwIHmeFFXA6xkP9gk2IfD8gQ&oe=6B033005\",\"media_type\":1,\"product_type\":\"feed\",\"user\":{\"pk\":\"51334541463\",\"username\":\"historyfeelsthepodcast\",\"id\":\"17841451283916817\"},\"id\":\"POLARIS_4032691962121249806\"}},{\"node\":{\"__typename\":\"XIGPolarisCarouselMedia\",\"pk\":\"4032685802882977960\",\"__isXIGPolarisMedia\":\"XIGPolarisCarouselMedia\",\"accessibility_caption\":\"Photo by historyfeelsthepodcast on November 17, 2026.\",\"caption\":{\"text\":\"Mansfield and Hargitay were among Hollywood\\u2019s most photographed couples of the late 1950s. Mansfield was then at the height of her fame, having starred in \\u201cThe Girl Can\\u2019t Help It\\u201d and \\u201cWill Success Spoil Rock Hunter?\\u201d Studios promoted her as a rival to Marilyn Monroe.\\n\\nHargitay was a Hungarian-born bodybuilder who had been crowned Mr. Universe in 1955. The two met in 1956, when he was performing in Mae West\\u2019s nightclub act.\\n\\nThey married in January 1958 and moved into a Beverly Hills mansion they called the Pink Palace, complete with a heart-shaped swimming pool. Hargitay, who had worked as a plumber and carpenter, helped build much of it himself.\\n\\nThey had three children together before divorcing in 1964. Their youngest daughter, Mariska Hargitay, later became an Emmy-winning actress best known for \\u201cLaw & Order: Special Victims Unit.\\u201d\\n\\nAdded fact: When Mansfield was killed in a car crash in 1967 at age 34, three of her children, including three-year-old Mariska, were asleep in the back seat and survived.\\n\\nSlide 2: The safety bar underneath the back of many semi-trailers is commonly called a \\u201cMansfield bar,\\u201d named after actress Jayne Mansfield. Her fatal 1967 crash, in which her car slid underneath a tractor-trailer, helped draw national attention to the danger of underride crashes.\"},\"code\":\"Df2-l8jmdCo\",\"seo_canonical_url\":null,\"display_uri\":\"https:\\/\\/scontent-dfw5-1.cdninstagram.com\\/v\\/t51.82787-15\\/912472647_18086994707709464_82278644150180412_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_e35_s640x640_tt6&_nc_cat=110&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ccb=7-5&_nc_sid=18de74&efg=eyJlZmdfdGFnIjoiQ0FST1VTRUxfSVRFTS5iZXN0X2ltYWdlX3VybGdlbi5DMyJ9&_nc_ohc=tC19t1-JLTAQ7kNvwG3eVQJ&_nc_oc=AdpCkdjU3rHAF4noWr7fxJogQhENF_t5WRMjpuKtyWeJseHc0CwJHwyW1FzDuKwHwrA&_nc_zt=23&_nc_ht=scontent-dfw5-1.cdninstagram.com&_nc_gid=2f9KpDZMNOTDwnWGLfg1Hg&_nc_ss=7c689&_nc_ct=RQosPBUKFQIAAAA&oh=00_AQQMkP60l7jyOimjjMCrXxQH06yOcS38AyH-mg9yxzDubg&oe=6B0312CF\",\"media_type\":8,\"product_type\":\"carousel_container\",\"user\":{\"pk\":\"51334541463\",\"username\":\"historyfeelsthepodcast\",\"id\":\"17841451283916817\"},\"carousel_media_count\":2,\"id\":\"POLARIS_4032685802882977960\"}},{\"node\":{\"__typename\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"pk\":\"4032618223354442365\",\"__isXIGPolarisMedia\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"accessibility_caption\":\"Photo by historyfeelsthepodcast on November 17, 2026. May be an image of text.\",\"caption\":{\"text\":\"During his presidency, from 1845 to 1849, the United States added Texas, the Oregon Territory and, after the Mexican-American War, California and most of the Southwest.\\n\\nHe had promised to serve only one term, and he kept that promise. He worked so relentlessly that he rarely took a day off, and by the time he left office in March 1849, he was exhausted and in poor health.\\n\\nOn June 15, 1849, he died of cholera at his home in Nashville. He was 53. It was the shortest retirement of any president in US history.\\n\\nSarah, who had been one of his closest political advisers, lived another 42 years. She wore black for the rest of her life and kept their home, Polk Place, much as he had left it, until her death in 1891.\\n\\nAdded fact: Polk was the first sitting president to be photographed inside the White House, in 1846, when a daguerreotype was taken of him with his cabinet.\"},\"code\":\"Df2vOiNzvJ9\",\"seo_canonical_url\":null,\"display_uri\":\"https:\\/\\/scontent-dfw5-3.cdninstagram.com\\/v\\/t51.82787-15\\/915850706_18086984915709464_1771567471133199293_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_e35_s640x640_tt6&_nc_cat=106&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ccb=7-5&_nc_sid=18de74&efg=eyJlZmdfdGFnIjoiRkVFRC5iZXN0X2ltYWdlX3VybGdlbi5DMyJ9&_nc_ohc=IPaPry-BUsoQ7kNvwHtv7Ri&_nc_oc=AdqtdE9--dkjdGzGway_9RIC3dhthYY0K-f9U6iu5nG8iYP5E4K7VJAtD1Uc6RDcl_Y&_nc_zt=23&_nc_ht=scontent-dfw5-3.cdninstagram.com&_nc_gid=2f9KpDZMNOTDwnWGLfg1Hg&_nc_ss=7c689&_nc_ct=RQosPBUKFQIAAAA&oh=00_AQTpwlMa53h2-V7U9b7EEAa0duMV98k-HP7SlqHGJs01gw&oe=6B03121D\",\"media_type\":1,\"product_type\":\"feed\",\"user\":{\"pk\":\"51334541463\",\"username\":\"historyfeelsthepodcast\",\"id\":\"17841451283916817\"},\"id\":\"POLARIS_4032618223354442365\"}},{\"node\":{\"__typename\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"pk\":\"4032617387681297923\",\"__isXIGPolarisMedia\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"accessibility_caption\":\"Photo by historyfeelsthepodcast on November 17, 2026. May be an image of xray, bone and text.\",\"caption\":{\"text\":\"R\\u00f6ntgen was a German physicist working at the University of W\\u00fcrzburg. On November 8, 1895, while experimenting with electrical currents in glass tubes, he noticed a screen across the room glowing, even though the tube was covered. Some kind of invisible ray was passing through solid material.\\n\\nHe called them \\u201cX-rays,\\u201d using \\u201cX\\u201d for something unknown.\\n\\nOn December 22, 1895, he took this image of his wife\\u2019s hand. It required an exposure of about 15 minutes. Her bones and wedding ring show up clearly, while the flesh around them is almost transparent.\\n\\nThe discovery spread around the world within weeks. Doctors immediately began using X-rays to find broken bones and bullets, and within a year, they were in use in hospitals across Europe and the United States.\\n\\nIn 1901, R\\u00f6ntgen won the very first Nobel Prize in Physics.\\n\\nAdded fact: R\\u00f6ntgen refused to patent his discovery, saying it belonged to all of humanity. He donated his Nobel Prize money to the University of W\\u00fcrzburg.\"},\"code\":\"Df2vCX7zrYD\",\"seo_canonical_url\":null,\"display_uri\":\"https:\\/\\/scontent-dfw6-2.cdninstagram.com\\/v\\/t51.82787-15\\/916698273_18086984780709464_2206249730322855044_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_e35_s640x640_tt6&_nc_cat=105&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ccb=7-5&_nc_sid=18de74&efg=eyJlZmdfdGFnIjoiRkVFRC5iZXN0X2ltYWdlX3VybGdlbi5DMyJ9&_nc_ohc=lr8KOdrBsvYQ7kNvwFwx-yH&_nc_oc=AdpQA-NvfVuUH71NqmkyQ7WDPiX_27SsJOhfxqVg_63Wj_LDI1JAhMIF3Y1AhjXejJY&_nc_zt=23&_nc_ht=scontent-dfw6-2.cdninstagram.com&_nc_gid=2f9KpDZMNOTDwnWGLfg1Hg&_nc_ss=7c689&_nc_ct=RQosPBUKFQIAAAA&oh=00_AQT2G9iko7j6CNvXPSuWcJmCgZxis_AgdYl9GQmABQOABg&oe=6B033B54\",\"media_type\":1,\"product_type\":\"feed\",\"user\":{\"pk\":\"51334541463\",\"username\":\"historyfeelsthepodcast\",\"id\":\"17841451283916817\"},\"id\":\"POLARIS_4032617387681297923\"}},{\"node\":{\"__typename\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"pk\":\"4032586645739924924\",\"__isXIGPolarisMedia\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"accessibility_caption\":\"Photo by historyfeelsthepodcast on November 17, 2026. May be an image of parking meter, sedan and text.\",\"caption\":{\"text\":\"The cars were Volvo 144 sedans, part of a much larger deal. In the early 1970s, Swedish companies hoped North Korea would become a big new market, and they shipped over mining equipment, machinery and other goods.\\n\\nAt the time, North Korea\\u2019s economy looked like it might be on the rise, and the country was eager to show off modern Western products.\\n\\nBut North Korea never paid. When global mineral prices crashed in the mid-1970s, its plans to pay with exports fell apart, and the debt was never settled.\\n\\nSweden\\u2019s export credit agency still sends a reminder twice a year. With interest, the amount has grown over the decades.\\n\\nMany of the Volvos stayed on the road for decades. Some were still used as taxis in Pyongyang in the 2000s and 2010s, like the ones seen here.\\n\\nAdded fact: In 1975, Sweden became the first Western country to open an embassy in North Korea. It still represents the interests of the United States, Canada and Australia there.\"},\"code\":\"Df2oDBRT-28\",\"seo_canonical_url\":null,\"display_uri\":\"https:\\/\\/scontent-dfw6-2.cdninstagram.com\\/v\\/t51.82787-15\\/912268852_18086978870709464_440729991128253655_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_e35_s640x640_tt6&_nc_cat=101&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ccb=7-5&_nc_sid=18de74&efg=eyJlZmdfdGFnIjoiRkVFRC5iZXN0X2ltYWdlX3VybGdlbi5DMyJ9&_nc_ohc=MkhSpPQFGMAQ7kNvwGwe0OQ&_nc_oc=AdrPv6TR-d8V6zYgQhz0RiuDTN3lv2BRBjEavD4zjBdTuoftr-ocUiVHdHS-HxI187A&_nc_zt=23&_nc_ht=scontent-dfw6-2.cdninstagram.com&_nc_gid=2f9KpDZMNOTDwnWGLfg1Hg&_nc_ss=7c689&_nc_ct=RQosPBUKFQIAAAA&oh=00_AQT7Ah-5tOmL4Vs2MCvFL_PnMJDQpjUDel2mXyC0J5jl3w&oe=6B0317CE\",\"media_type\":1,\"product_type\":\"feed\",\"user\":{\"pk\":\"51334541463\",\"username\":\"historyfeelsthepodcast\",\"id\":\"17841451283916817\"},\"id\":\"POLARIS_4032586645739924924\"}},{\"node\":{\"__typename\":\"XIGPolarisCarouselMedia\",\"pk\":\"4032569764485611183\",\"__isXIGPolarisMedia\":\"XIGPolarisCarouselMedia\",\"accessibility_caption\":\"Photo by historyfeelsthepodcast on November 17, 2026.\",\"caption\":{\"text\":\"That summer, 21-year-old Charles and his sister, Princess Anne, made a visit to Washington as guests of President Richard Nixon. Their schedule included a cruise on the Potomac, a trip to Camp David and a Washington Senators baseball game.\\n\\nThe press quickly suspected matchmaking. Tricia was 24, single and often called America\\u2019s closest thing to a princess, and reporters noticed how often the two were seated together. Nixon was rumored to be hoping for a royal romance.\\n\\nNothing came of it. Charles reportedly found Tricia polished but stiff, and years later, accounts suggested he hadn\\u2019t enjoyed the trip much at all.\\n\\nLess than a year later, in June 1971, Tricia married Edward Cox in the White House Rose Garden, the first outdoor wedding ever held at the White House. Charles married Lady Diana Spencer a decade later and became king in 2022.\\n\\nAdded fact: The Washington Senators didn\\u2019t stay much longer either. After the 1971 season, the team moved to Texas and became the Rangers, leaving Washington without a Major League team until the Nationals arrived in 2005.\"},\"code\":\"Df2kNXYJT6v\",\"seo_canonical_url\":null,\"display_uri\":\"https:\\/\\/scontent-dfw5-2.cdninstagram.com\\/v\\/t51.82787-15\\/910781958_18086976062709464_4041917824927007107_n.jpg?stp=dst-jpg_e35_s640x640_tt6&_nc_cat=103&_nc_map=urlgen_bucketless&ccb=7-5&_nc_sid=18de74&efg=eyJlZmdfdGFnIjoiQ0FST1VTRUxfSVRFTS5iZXN0X2ltYWdlX3VybGdlbi5DMyJ9&_nc_ohc=qtjvTlpCuIMQ7kNvwEIeAJB&_nc_oc=AdpV93rDYUFpTjbn6IzoTEyWBkT68pUNFGQfHPLDJca3A9KPvZ9Ggu_gqf4y-6hsrro&_nc_zt=23&_nc_ht=scontent-dfw5-2.cdninstagram.com&_nc_gid=2f9KpDZMNOTDwnWGLfg1Hg&_nc_ss=7c689&_nc_ct=RQosPBUKFQIAAAA&oh=00_AQTUVERoGYeujyF_RRnuq-KT5RXCcM3VZI1b28tQZyMdPA&oe=6B031C8D\",\"media_type\":8,\"product_type\":\"carousel_container\",\"user\":{\"pk\":\"51334541463\",\"username\":\"historyfeelsthepodcast\",\"id\":\"17841451283916817\"},\"carousel_media_count\":2,\"id\":\"POLARIS_4032569764485611183\"}},{\"node\":{\"__typename\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"pk\":\"4032525672832160521\",\"__isXIGPolarisMedia\":\"XIGPolarisImageMedia\",\"accessibility_caption\":\"Photo by historyfeelsthepodcast on November 17, 2026. May be an image of text.\",\"caption\":{\"text\":\"The Quonset hut was designed in 1941 at Quonset Point, Rhode Island, for the US Navy. It was a curved, prefabricated steel building that was cheap, light and could be put up in a day. More than 150,000",
